<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Hill of Devi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1953</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hill of Devi is a book by the English novelist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/e-m-forster">E. M. Forster</a>, published in 1953. It is built from his letters home from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dewas">Dewas</a> Senior, which he visited in 1912–1913 and where in 1921 he served as private secretary to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> Tukoji Rao III. The book gives an affectionate and comic picture of court life in a small Indian princely state and sheds light on his novel A Passage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p></div>
